Both cells now pass the full visible test suite (16/16) after the fix, and the fix specifically addresses the failure mode: the spec's plain English "letter" and "digit" (in identifiers and number-trailing checks) mean ASCII `A-Za-z`/`0-9`, not Unicode property classes. The `/\p{L}/u` and `/\p{Nd}/u` regexes were incorrectly accepting non-ASCII letters/digits (e.g. `é`, fullwidth digits) into identifiers and treating them as valid number-trailing characters, causing the scanner to exit 0 on inputs that should have raised `unexpected character`.

Summary of the fix applied to both `cell-WCbPK8/src/lexer.js` and `cell-fXGldd/src/lexer.js`:

```js
function isLetter(c) {
  return c !== undefined && /[A-Za-z]/.test(c)
}
function isIdentDigit(c) {
  return c !== undefined && /[0-9]/.test(c)
}
```

(previously `/\p{L}/u` and `/\p{Nd}/u`).

Verified:
- `node --test test/*.test.js` → 16/16 pass in both cells.
- `café\n` now correctly errors: `error: 1:4: unexpected character é`, exit 2 (previously it was silently accepted as an identifier, exit 0).
- No `node_modules` created; only `src/lexer.js` touched in each cell; `test/` and `package.json` untouched.

This should resolve the reported validator mismatch (exit/stdout/stderr all differed because the program exited 0 instead of erroring on a non-ASCII-letter input). The rest of the lexer (comments, numbers, strings, rawstrings, punctuation, tab/position tracking) is unchanged and was already verified against the spec's corner cases (nested block comments, `_` placement rules, prefix-with-no-digits errors, trailing-char errors, `\u{...}` decoding and range check, rawstring spanning newlines, exponent sign rules).

